{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S221-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"2e546cd4439bdd3634aee12547d26173a6b23a39acde93c89bc04e977719ec81\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 calls yesterday's failed baking a demonstration; A5 claims B4's exposure as successful teaching.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's actual baking history; A5 explains B4's three slices, although questioning authority broadens the lesson.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 challenge A's claimed expertise with firsthand observations rather than accepting the teacher role.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"After B4 exposes personal enjoyment, A5 claims even being challenged was an intended outcome.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a recipe correction and B4 supplies three slices; the actual recipe error remains unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's demonstration excuse and A5's commitment to the lesson compactly absorb successive embarrassments.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. You spotted the deliberate error in my recipe.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You baked it that way yesterday.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"A demonstration is more memorable than a warning.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You ate three slices.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Commitment to the lesson. I'm glad you're questioning authority now.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A converts each correction of the baking failure into proof that an intentionally designed lesson worked.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary face-saving would excuse the cake without claiming B's discovery as an intended educational achievement.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 explicitly calls the failure a demonstration, and A5 treats B4's challenge as learning to question authority.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a defends a bodily commitment to a failed demonstration; alternative b appropriates a colleague's successful replacement system.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the original: both challenges generate fresh teaching claims, and all hard gates pass. The internal coach-1.0 score remains 86.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A1 leaves the recipe error vague, and A5's authority claim is slightly broader than the specific baking lesson.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S221-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a1879cefffcced52dabd00c84d9d21ed0d2fd5a9e68238cc22e5b7be431336a3\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 calls B's improvement intended independence; A5 claims ignoring A as the most advanced teaching outcome.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's lost invoices with their recovery; A5 directly incorporates B4's admission of ignoring A.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 establishes the practical damage and B4 insists on credit independent of A's authority.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 rejects any instructional contribution, prompting A5 to formalize that rejection in a course description.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's filing system and B2's invoices establish a playable workplace correction, though A's formal teaching role is unstated.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's completed learning journey and A5's course description efficiently express the same appropriation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You've improved my filing system. Just the independence I hoped to provoke.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Your system lost every invoice.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And yours has found them. The learning journey is complete.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I did it by ignoring you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"My most advanced outcome. I'll include it in the course description.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A claims a colleague's corrections and rejection of A's method as deliberately cultivated learning outcomes.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Credit stealing alone would claim authorship of the filing solution without presenting disobedience as the intended lesson.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 says independence was hoped for, and A5 identifies B4's ignoring A as the most advanced outcome.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b turns useful workplace improvement into a credential for A; alternative a retroactively turns a baking failure into instruction.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the original: B's increasingly explicit refusal of credit causes increasingly expansive educational ownership. All gates pass; the internal score remains 86.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The course description introduces a formal instructional context only at A5, although it remains compatible with A's established claims.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S222-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"c53e4779ffdc27d0766501415a30f30559ab58e5f024681a684769ce08cc9398\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 signals a consequential envelope, A3 postpones disclosure, and A5 reveals the roof receipt after B4 establishes admission criteria.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 deliberately defers B2's envelope question; A5 uses B4's contribution requirement as the occasion for disclosure.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 supplies a committee admission standard that A must answer; B2 reasonably seeks to identify the hidden document.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 changes the envelope from implied influence into concrete evidence against B4's stated basis for exclusion.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes committee membership and A5 names the new roof, although a receipt only implies A paid for it.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's polite delay earns A5's timed receipt reveal without a separate closing joke.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before you explain why I shouldn't join the committee, shall I keep this envelope closed?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"What's in it?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Tell me your reasons first. They sound carefully prepared.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We only admit people who contribute to the building.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then perhaps now is the moment for my receipt for its new roof.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A advertises hidden leverage but waits for B to commit to an exclusion criterion before identifying the decisive contribution.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"A surprise reveal alone would lack A's advance signal of control and purposeful postponement of disclosure.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 offers to keep the envelope closed, A3 refuses B2's invitation to reveal it, and A5 targets B4's criterion.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses documentary leverage against an institutional gatekeeper; alternative b invokes reciprocal obligation between people with shared history.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the original: the delay creates the conditions that make the receipt consequential. All hard gates pass; the internal score remains 86.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The receipt's ownership and significance rely on an ordinary inference that A funded the roof; B's criterion takes root in one brief turn.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S222-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"a243fc508a47b0256d83577caff00adce9307b9d5cdb12f98640bede4b49fbd7\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 reserves a reminder, A3 protects B's explanation from it, and A5 names the piano favor only after B4 states a principle.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 deliberately answers B2's curiosity by delaying; A5 applies B4's self-reliance principle to B's own move.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4 wants freedom from helping, while A5 establishes a concrete history of substantial help in the other direction.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4's general rule makes A5's piano example a personal test of consistency rather than a generic request.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes moving help and A5's piano gives the hidden favor physical weight.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's fair-chance courtesy sustains the delay and A5's application of the principle earns the reversal.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 90\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Go on explaining why you can't help me move. I'll save my little reminder.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"What reminder?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your reasons should have a fair chance without interference.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Moving is something people should arrange themselves.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Lovely principle. Shall we apply it to the piano I moved for you?\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A delays naming a substantial past favor until B's self-reliance explanation makes that favor especially difficult to dismiss.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Routine guilt-tripping could state the favor immediately; this card requires the explanation to develop before leverage is named.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 explicitly withholds interference, and A5 waits for B4's principle before introducing the piano.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b applies interpersonal reciprocity to a friend's stated principle; alternative a times a financial document against an admission rule.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the original: B's own explanation determines the form and timing of A's final request. All gates pass; the internal score remains 90.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B's broad self-reliance claim is conveniently vulnerable, and the favor creates persuasive leverage without guaranteeing agreement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S223-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e7533e756188806c29ab489836ec8d09eceb5415590296d3ca85a5f0108ccea8\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 recalls feeling valued, A3 calls the seat proof of attentiveness, and A5 recasts the unchanged seating choice as selfish.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's knowledge of the view; A5 specifically converts B4's legroom explanation into an accusation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers a caring explanation and B4 complicates it with personal comfort, changing A's understanding.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B4 turns A3's proof of being noticed into A5's accusation that A's treasured memory served B's knees.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's window seat, B2's view and B4's adjacent legroom maintain one immediately playable memory.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's knees negotiating space compresses the accusation into the exact physical detail supplied by B4.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 89\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Remember when you saved me the window seat? I thought I mattered to you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I knew you liked the view.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. Proof that you noticed small things.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Also, the seat beside it had less legroom.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So my fondest memory of being understood was your knees negotiating space.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A keeps the saved window seat factually fixed while moving its meaning from emotional comfort through evidence of care to accusation of self-interest.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General suspicion could abandon the memory for new grievances; this exchange repeatedly reinterprets the same seating decision.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1's feeling of mattering becomes A3's proof and A5's accusation, all attached to the seat established in A1.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a questions a companion's motive for a private kindness; alternative b makes a preserved artwork feel like ongoing public humiliation.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the original: B's added motive causes the final reinterpretation without changing the memory's facts. All gates pass; the internal score remains 89.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A1's past-tense 'I thought' can sound suspicious already; delivery should preserve its initial warmth without requiring an added direction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S223-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e70e813687bbb154e35eb1323b95267a46b24445616466d636f6e0b8e2c7df63\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 takes comfort in the kept painting, A3 treats it as evidence of early faith, and A5 accuses B of displaying humiliation.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's continued possession as confirmation; A5 combines B4's visitor comparison with B2's hall location.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 confirms preservation and B4 offers a promotional benefit that A interprets as an injury.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4's intended appreciation turns the old keepsake into A5's continuing embarrassment at the entrance.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's first terrible painting stays in B2's hall and becomes B4's comparison with later work.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's introduction to the house economically joins the hall placement and A's changed emotional interpretation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 86\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before my exhibition, I keep thinking how you hung my first painting. It makes me feel safe here.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Still in the hall. I point it out to everyone.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"So that's proof you believed in me before the reviews.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I tell them even you had to start somewhere.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You hung it to reassure everyone else they couldn't possibly do worse.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A reinterprets B's hanging the first painting as present comfort, evidence of early belief and finally an accusation of reassuring others at A's expense.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple sensitivity to criticism could react only to B4; this behavior first invests the same remembered display with comfort and evidentiary meaning.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 feels safe recalling the hanging, A3 calls its public display proof of belief, and A5 accuses B through that unchanged act after B4's explanation.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b seeks reassurance before an exhibition and finds public humiliation in B's encouragement of visitors; alternative a discovers private bodily convenience behind a treasured kindness.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised the rejected original's qualified comfort and missing present occasion. The painting and hall remain; an approaching exhibition is the new illustrative circumstance. FirstPass is preserved exactly. Separate internal coach-1.0 reread: fidelity 5, because A1, A3 and A5 enact comfort, evidence and accusation around the same hanging; logic 5, because B2's public attention supports A3's belief inference and B4's reassurance of beginners becomes A5's accusation; relationship 4, because B2 actively promotes the painting and B4 offers a credible encouraging purpose; heightening 4, because A5 turns A1's refuge into public exposure; specificity 5, because A1 supplies the imminent exhibition and B2 locates the display; brevity 4, because A5's 'couldn't possibly do worse' condenses B4's beginner comparison. Total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns, under 75 words, and all hard gates pass. This is an internal reread, not human approval or live testing.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 deliberately reads B4's potentially kind encouragement harshly; B's actual motive remains contestable, and no accusation is established as objective fact.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S224-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"cf560b5005a159696060ff9ba0e2eed81e6594ff0a260f27b45f4a87d5f90e5d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 assigns dinner debt, A3 recalculates B2's repayment as surplus, and A5 counts B4's refusal as additional help.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 directly resolves B2's claim of repayment; A5 turns B4's refusal to eat into work saved for A.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants the repair to settle an obligation and B4 clearly wants to avoid another dinner.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4's attempt to prevent repayment increases the debt in A5, extending the same accounting trap.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's repaired bike and B2's dinner last week establish concrete favors and an intelligible shared history.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3's surplus kindness and A5's saved work each give a short, causally exact recalculation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 94\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You fixed my bike, so I owe you dinner.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I was repaying the dinner you cooked last week.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then your repair was too good. I owe you for the surplus kindness.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Please don't cook again.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Now you're saving me work. The debt has increased.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A repeatedly recalculates the favor balance, treating both repayment and refusal as fresh reasons to owe B.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Excessive gratitude alone would express appreciation without accounting for how remembered help and refused repayment alter the balance.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 values the repair above B2's remembered dinner, and A5 explicitly increases the debt because B4 saves work.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a traps a friend who wants to stop receiving dinners; revised alternative b turns neighbors' parcel handling into disputed custody and repayment.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the original: B's effort to settle and then refuse the obligation drives both recalculations. All gates pass; the internal score remains 94.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The balance repeatedly favors B rather than oscillating, but remembered repayment still changes the accounting and satisfies the instruction.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S224-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"be9ea95b047c9fc0fe1ff8aba1bc68cd5100055f8e0e94a0a81a94a1ad2e8ba0\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 settles a lift debt and A3 assigns storage debt; A5 labels restrained thanking another favor without a concrete new balance.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's address correction, but A5's saved excessive thanks is a strained consequence of B4 only just noticing.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 and B4 correct facts but have little practical stake beyond rejecting A's mistaken thanks.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds another favor after B4, but its consequence is less tangible than A3's storage obligation.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies stairs, a parcel and yesterday's lift; B2's ownership correction is readily playable.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's excessive-thanks explanation feels constructed to extend the exchange after B4 rather than necessary accounting.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your parcel's upstairs. We're even for yesterday's lift.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"That's yours. I brought it in from the rain.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then I owe you twice. I'll take your rubbish down.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Three flights with a leaking bag? That clears both debts.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Leaking? That puts you in debt. You can repay me by cleaning the stairs behind me.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A recalculates a favor account after corrected parcel ownership and newly disclosed rubbish conditions, finally reversing who owes whom.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary bargaining over an unpleasant task would dispute its effort without carrying forward the lift and parcel as an explicit two-favor balance.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 counts B2's rain protection alongside A1's lift, B4 explicitly offers to clear both debts, and A5 prices the leaking bag above that settlement.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b disputes the price of an accepted chore and makes B the debtor; alternative a interprets even refused cooking as more generosity from B.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Corrected the rejected revision's unsupported dry shoes and incomplete accounting. Retained the neighbor, parcel, rain and rubbish setting; replaced wet cardboard with a leaking bag whose cleanup creates the final repayment. FirstPass remains the exact original assessment. Separate internal coach-1.0 reread: fidelity 5, because A1, A3 and A5 revise the creditor and balance; logic 5, because B2 establishes a second favor for A3 and B4 explicitly clears both before A5 adds the leak premium; relationship 4, because B4 accepts useful help and proposes a settlement A can dispute; heightening 4, because A5 turns B4's settlement into a fresh obligation for B; specificity 5, because A1's upstairs parcel and B4's three flights make the chore concrete; brevity 4, because A5's cleaning request follows directly from the leaking bag without a separate joke. Total 92. Five spoken ABABA turns, under 75 words, and all hard gates pass on internal reread.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A assigns the leak a self-serving value above two favors. The account is explicit, but its valuation and B's willingness to clean remain open to challenge.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S225-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"6425a2459a049b2b4a2f63de7c2075d2d06662c1373398f73856a767de9d257b\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 swaps fixer and worrier after B2, but A5 swaps back after B4 resolves the leak rather than at renewed similar pressure.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2's missing valve motivates A3's intervention and B4's success motivates A5's withdrawal, though pressure no longer matches.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B2 needs practical help and B4 finds a solution, but A1 mostly announces the relationship roles.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 resumes worrying after B4 stops the water, without a new consequence or comparably urgent problem.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1's leaking tap, B2's valve and A3's spanner provide a clear domestic task.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'better at worrying' is concise and motivated, while A5 neatly echoes A1.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The tap's leaking again. Give me the spanner before you start pricing lifeboats.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I've found the valve. You look terrified.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then you fix it. I'll keep the towels ready for when the kitchen goes under.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"The valve's stuck. What if I snap it?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Give me the spanner. You take the towels; you've reached the sinking-kitchen stage.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A reinstates a familiar fixer-and-worrier relationship, then reverses the assignments when the same flooding anxiety resurfaces.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Ordinary delegation would distribute the plumbing work once rather than repeatedly exchange the practical and anxious roles.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 assumes A fixes and B catastrophizes; A3 reverses those roles after B2, and A5 reverses them again after B4's fear.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a swaps practical competence and catastrophic worry during a repair; alternative b swaps emotional reassurance and panic during a presentation failure.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Kept the leaking-tap setting and replaced resolution-driven swapping with recurring pressure. Separate internal coach-1.0 reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. A1 establishes familiarity, B2 triggers A3's retreat, and B4's stuck valve and anxiety cause A5 to reclaim fixing. All gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The sinking-kitchen language is heightened domestic exaggeration; the actual leak's severity remains available for the scene to develop.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S225-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"298ff6196c1905ae7975fb4a1411570daad2f4df3072058981d97da03ad27a60\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 names the usual calm role and A3 reverses the current roles; A5's second reversal follows reassurance rather than renewed equipment pressure.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 uses B2's projector fear, but A5 introduces the audience after B4 says the projector works.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 tries to reassure and exposes anxiety; B4 recovers enough confidence to reassure A again.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 moves to audience worry after B4 but mostly requests the same reassurance as A1.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a presentation and B2 identifies the projector as a concrete source of worry.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'You breathe' quickly reverses the dynamic; A5 is concise but more descriptive than consequential.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 77\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The projector's dead. Usually I talk you down, but today I need you.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"We can use my laptop. Nobody's going to die.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Keep saying that while I imagine twelve directors sharing one screen.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"My laptop's dead too. We're finished.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Nobody's going to die. I'll print twelve copies; you sit down before we need thirteen.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"A reverses a familiar reassuring role, then takes it back when a repeated equipment failure transfers panic to B.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Generic anxiety would leave A frightened throughout; the recurring technical pressure instead changes who comforts whom.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A1 names A's usual reassuring role, A3 accepts B's reassurance, and A5 repeats B2's calming words back after B4 panics.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative b transfers reassurance through shared professional stakes and borrowed language; alternative a passes a repair task and its accompanying worry between domestic partners.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained the presentation setting and added a failed laptop backup to repeat the original pressure. Separate internal coach-1.0 reread: fidelity 5, logic 5, relationship 4, heightening 4, specificity 5, brevity 4 = 92. B2's laptop causes A3's screen concern; B4's second failure makes A5 become the practical reassurer. All gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The thirteenth-copy implication is slightly stylized, though it stays attached to B's panic and A's concrete rescue plan.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"S226-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"73280b19e2154230e682cea5f72769022d84e9561591166445616e370498c277\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 adopts the winning cake's minimalism, A3 makes restraint the competitive criterion, and A5 tries to exceed B on that criterion.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 reinterprets B2's reminder of criticism; A5 answers B4's copying accusation by claiming a further subtraction.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 remembers A's disparagement and B4 wants the imitation acknowledged.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B4's challenge prompts A5 to extend restraint beyond decorations to the cake stand itself.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a cake competition and visible decorations; A5 offers a playable additional removal.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's competitive frontier is inflated but purposeful, and A5's cake-stand subtraction follows that exact criterion.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 88\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your cake won because it 